interesting an addition, but a possible choice.
It bypasses the fairly tricky (by design, I suspect)
current methods.

> I also think it's too powerful. My fix would be to
> make the healing spell useless on iron golems. :)

Nah, there's too many other non-biological/non
"living"(*) pets to degrade the healing spell that
way, that's unbalancing the other direction, making
a big shift in which pets are desirable.

Instead, this use for healing spells should really
_hurt_, it shouldn't be something one does casually.

So, make the PC expend a potion of (ordinary, extra,
full) healing along with the spell of healing (a
"material component" as it were), and then degrade
the chances of success probablilistically, so that
expert spell use plus blessed full healing get
highest chance of success, and highest damage repair
(rolled separately) while basic spell use and cursed
ordinary healing get nearly no chance of success and
minimal repair level chances if successful. That all
implements as essentially a three dimensional lookup
table or two: spellcasting expertise X potion type X
potion BUC state X [spell success table, damage
repair base amount table].

